WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:10.400
Should tech platforms be liable for what
users post online? In 1996, Congress said

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.190
they shouldn't. Section 230 of the
Communications Decency Act protects

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.000
platforms from lawsuits based on
user-generated content. A person who says

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.539
they were harmed by material posted
online has to go after whoever posted it,

00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:25.710
not the platform on which it was posted.
But with controversy swirling around big

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:30.179
tech, some are arguing that CDA 230
should go away. That would probably be a

00:00:30.179 --> 00:00:35.430
mistake. Without CDA 230, platforms might
either ban user-generated content, or

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.550
refuse to moderate it because of the
risk of being treated as an author or

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:43.260
publisher. Lawsuits could swamp their
small competitors. If we could go back in

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.620
time, it might have been better to let
common law courts determine the rules. A

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.039
common rule of no liability for
platforms would have been better than

00:00:50.039 --> 00:00:54.600
immunity given by Congress, because that
will be a political football forever. CDA

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.149
230
gave us the huge, vibrant social media

00:00:57.149 --> 00:01:01.230
environment we've got today, even though
some bad actors get a pass. We should

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.830
probably keep that sensible liability
rule rather than experimenting with

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.720
something new.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.719
What do you think about CDA 230? Should

00:01:09.719 --> 00:01:14.579
Congress reform, it repeal it, or leave it
in place? Let us know in our poll. Also,

00:01:14.579 --> 00:01:18.979
let us know what other topics you'd like
